# Independently authored PG USER / ROLE grammar-leak +
# hand-rolled-formatter sentinel slice for the GPL MTR-
# format runner. NOT copied or adapted from MySQL's
# mysql-test/ or PG regress.
#
# Adjacent companion slices (do NOT duplicate):
#   - auth_basic_extra.test pins the bare-form known
#     gaps:
#       * line 93:  CREATE ROLE r1
#       * line 98:  CREATE ROLE r2 WITH LOGIN
#       * line 103: DROP ROLE r1
#       * line 108: DROP ROLE IF EXISTS no_such
#       * line 118: CREATE USER u1 (bare)
#       * line 128: DROP USER u1
#       * line 138: ALTER USER u1 IDENTIFIED BY 'newpw'
#       * line 143: ALTER ROLE r1 WITH PASSWORD 'newpw'
#     The bare-form parse-reject is ALREADY pinned by
#     errno 1064; this slice does NOT re-pin those exact
#     spellings. It pins the STRUCTURE of the four
#     rejection patterns and the MESSAGE-SHAPE
#     fingerprints (Line/Column suffix vs hand-rolled
#     bespoke formatter), which auth_basic_extra does
#     NOT capture.
#   - pg_pubsub_replication_sentinel:240 pins
#     `CREATE ROLE r REPLICATION` (shape c, attribute-
#     keyword variant). This slice covers DIFFERENT
#     PG role attributes (LOGIN, NOLOGIN, PASSWORD,
#     SUPERUSER, CREATEDB, CREATEROLE, BYPASSRLS,
#     ENCRYPTED, VALID UNTIL, CONNECTION LIMIT, IN ROLE,
#     IN GROUP, ROLE, ADMIN, RENAME TO, SET search_path,
#     ALTER ROLE ALL).
#   - pg_fdw_inherits_sentinel:159-161 pins
#     `CREATE USER MAPPING FOR ...` (the FDW composite
#     that sqlparser's CREATE USER arm consumes). This
#     slice covers the SIMPLE-FORM `CREATE USER <name>
#     <option> ...` grammar, not the MAPPING composite.
#
#   Pre-author dedup grep on tests/main/*.test for
#   `CREATE USER`, `CREATE ROLE`, `ALTER USER`,
#   `ALTER ROLE`, `DROP USER`, `DROP ROLE`: hits as
#   above. Per [[feedback_gap_scan_must_grep_all_files]]
#   the case-insensitive grep was the full population.
#
# This slice pins FOUR structural findings:
#
#   FINDING 1 — CROSS-DIALECT MySQL GRAMMAR LEAK in
#   CREATE USER. zeta's sqlparser fork has the MySQL
#   CREATE USER grammar (`name = value` option pairs),
#   NOT PG's `name OPTION value [OPTION value ...]`
#   grammar. Every PG option keyword after the user
#   name (PASSWORD, SUPERUSER, NOSUPERUSER, CREATEDB,
#   CREATEROLE, LOGIN, NOLOGIN, ENCRYPTED, VALID UNTIL,
#   CONNECTION LIMIT, IN ROLE, IN GROUP, ROLE, ADMIN,
#   BYPASSRLS) is rejected with `Expected: =, found:
#   <token>` at the column where the PG option keyword
#   appears. Most damning: an UNKNOWN identifier like
#   `NOSUCHOPT 'x'` STILL produces `Expected: =, found:
#   'x'` — proving the `= value` requirement is at the
#   GRAMMAR layer, not a per-keyword registry. The
#   parser will accept ANY identifier as an option name
#   as long as it is followed by `=` and a value. This
#   is parallel to PR #452's Hive/Spark contamination
#   of the ALTER TABLE whitelist:
#   [[project_two_layer_type_recognition_pattern]] —
#   wrong dialect's grammar leaked into the PG-facing
#   surface. CORRUPTION-class for PG `CREATE USER` DDL
#   coming from migration scripts or pg_dump output.
#
#   Compare with PR #459's CREATE DATABASE silent-
#   accept of `NOSUCHOPT 'x'` (no grammar requirement
#   at all — the parser drops unknown options
#   silently). CREATE DATABASE and CREATE USER occupy
#   OPPOSITE EXTREMES of the parse-time validation
#   spectrum: CREATE DATABASE swallows nonsense without
#   complaint; CREATE USER demands MySQL grammar even
#   for nonsense option names.
#
#   FINDING 2 — CREATE ROLE / ALTER USER / ALTER ROLE
#   shape (c) catch-all. The ENTIRE statement family —
#   CREATE ROLE with any KNOWN PG attribute keyword,
#   ALTER USER WITH PASSWORD, ALTER USER RENAME TO,
#   ALTER USER SET search_path, ALTER ROLE WITH
#   NOLOGIN, and the PG14+ ALTER ROLE ALL SET <param>
#   — is routed through the shape (c) catch-all:
#   `unsupported statement: sqlparser::ast::Statement`
#   (the Debug repr of the AST variant, per
#   [[project_function_arms_gap_closed]]). zeta has
#   ZERO executor arms for the ROLE-management AST
#   variants. Migration risk: every PG role attribute
#   change silently fails the same way and is
#   indistinguishable from one another at the error-
#   string layer.
#
#   FINDING 3 — KW-vs-UNKNOWN-IDENTIFIER ASYMMETRY in
#   CREATE ROLE / ALTER USER. Within the CREATE ROLE /
#   ALTER USER family there is a TWO-STAGE asymmetry:
#   KNOWN PG keywords (LOGIN, NOLOGIN, RENAME, SET,
#   PASSWORD) route through the sqlparser AST and hit
#   shape (c); UNKNOWN identifiers (`NOSUCHOPT`) hit
#   the parser-layer whitelist FIRST and reject with
#   `Expected: end of statement, found: NOSUCHOPT at
#   Line: 1, Column: N`. Two different code paths for
#   what looks like the same syntactic position:
#   ROLE-attribute slot. Cross-link
#   [[project_two_layer_type_recognition_pattern]] —
#   parser-arm vs dispatcher-arm divergence again.
#
#   NOTE the THIRD asymmetry within CREATE USER: there
#   the UNKNOWN identifier follows the MySQL grammar
#   (Finding 1) and `NOSUCHOPT 'x'` rejects with
#   `Expected: =`. Three distinct error paths for
#   "unknown option in PG user/role DDL", split by
#   statement keyword. Pinning the asymmetry is the
#   point of this slice.
#
#   FINDING 4 — DROP ROLE / DROP USER HAND-ROLLED
#   FORMATTERS (4th and 5th witnesses of the
#   [[project_hand_rolled_error_formatters]] pattern).
#   `DROP ROLE r1` errors with `unsupported DROP ROLE
#   statement` and `DROP USER u1` errors with
#   `unsupported DROP USER statement` — neither
#   carries the `at Line: N, Column: M` suffix that
#   sqlparser appends to its whitelist errors. These
#   are bespoke formatters parallel to:
#     * PR #455's `unsupported DROP STAGE statement`
#       (DROP-family bespoke formatter #1)
#     * PR #452's ALTER TABLE Hive/Spark whitelist
#       formatter
#     * PR #457's `Expecting DELETE ROWS, PRESERVE
#       ROWS or DROPNOSUCHOPT` smushed-typo for ON
#       COMMIT
#     * PR #458's `CREATE FUNCTION requires a function
#       body (AS $$ ... $$)`
#   This brings the cluster of known hand-rolled error
#   paths to FIVE — three of them in the DROP family
#   (STAGE, ROLE, USER). Each carries its own risk of
#   typo / drift / formatting inconsistency since none
#   of them is generated by sqlparser. Error-string
#   matchers that key off the `at Line:` suffix will
#   NOT match these errors.
#
# Big-picture finding:
#
#   The PG user/role DDL surface is fragmented across
#   FOUR distinct parser/dispatcher behaviors:
#     - CREATE USER: cross-dialect MySQL grammar leak
#       — every option position demands `= value`
#       (Finding 1).
#     - CREATE ROLE / ALTER USER / ALTER ROLE with
#       KNOWN PG keywords: shape (c) catch-all
#       (Finding 2).
#     - CREATE ROLE / ALTER USER with UNKNOWN
#       identifier: parser-whitelist reject with
#       Line/Column (Finding 3).
#     - DROP USER / DROP ROLE: bespoke hand-rolled
#       formatter without Line/Column (Finding 4).
#   The same statement family takes four code paths.
#   Pinning the boundary between them is the point of
#   this slice.
#
# Wire divergence check: probed via mysqlwire (MTR
# canonical, dynamic port reservation). Per
# [[feedback_probe_wire_match_slice_wire]] pgwire was
# NOT separately probed — error messages are stable
# across clients for parse-layer rejects.
#
# Adaptation notes:
#   # zeta-adapt: CROSS-DIALECT MySQL GRAMMAR LEAK —
#     PG `CREATE USER u PASSWORD 'pw'` errors 1064
#     `Expected: =, found: 'pw' at Line: 1, Column:
#     25`. Use `CREATE USER u (placeholder)` only if
#     no options are needed; otherwise the statement
#     CANNOT be expressed in zeta. Strip from
#     migration scripts.
#   # zeta-adapt: KNOWN GAP — `CREATE ROLE r LOGIN
#     PASSWORD 'pw'` errors 1064 `unsupported
#     statement: sqlparser::ast::Statement` via the
#     shape (c) catch-all. Same for `CREATE ROLE r
#     NOLOGIN`, `ALTER USER u WITH PASSWORD 'newpw'`,
#     `ALTER USER u RENAME TO u2`, `ALTER USER u SET
#     search_path = pg_catalog`, `ALTER ROLE r WITH
#     NOLOGIN`, `ALTER ROLE ALL SET <param> = <val>`.
#     All silently fail at the dispatcher with the
#     same error message; do not key off the message
#     to distinguish them.
#   # zeta-adapt: ASYMMETRY — `CREATE ROLE r
#     NOSUCHOPT` errors 1064 `Expected: end of
#     statement, found: NOSUCHOPT at Line: 1, Column:
#     16` (parser whitelist reject), DIFFERENT from
#     the shape (c) message above. Same asymmetry for
#     `ALTER USER u NOSUCHOPT 'x'`. Migration tooling
#     that maps known-keyword vs unknown-identifier
#     errors should expect different messages.
#   # zeta-adapt: HAND-ROLLED ERROR PATHS — `DROP ROLE
#     <name>` errors 1064 `unsupported DROP ROLE
#     statement` and `DROP USER <name>` errors 1064
#     `unsupported DROP USER statement`. NEITHER
#     carries the `at Line: N, Column: M` suffix.
#     Error-string matchers that key off the Line/
#     Column suffix will NOT match.

--echo === PATTERN alpha — CREATE USER MySQL `= value` grammar leak (12 rejects) ===

# Every PG CREATE USER option keyword demands `=
# <value>` after the user name. The Most damning row
# is u13 NOSUCHOPT 'x' — even a fabricated identifier
# is accepted as an option name, then the `= value`
# requirement still applies. The grammar layer is
# MySQL `name = value` pairs, not the PG
# `name OPTION value` form.

--error 1064
CREATE USER u1 PASSWORD 'pw';

--error 1064
CREATE USER u2 SUPERUSER PASSWORD 'pw';

--error 1064
CREATE USER u3 NOSUPERUSER CREATEDB CREATEROLE LOGIN PASSWORD 'pw';

--error 1064
CREATE USER u4 WITH PASSWORD 'pw';

--error 1064
CREATE USER u5 ENCRYPTED PASSWORD 'pw';

--error 1064
CREATE USER u6 VALID UNTIL '2030-01-01';

--error 1064
CREATE USER u7 CONNECTION LIMIT 5;

--error 1064
CREATE USER u8 IN ROLE r1;

--error 1064
CREATE USER u9 IN GROUP r1;

--error 1064
CREATE USER u10 ROLE r1;

--error 1064
CREATE USER u11 ADMIN r1;

--error 1064
CREATE USER u12 BYPASSRLS;

--error 1064
CREATE USER u13 NOSUCHOPT 'x';

--echo === PATTERN beta — CREATE ROLE shape (c) catch-all + NOSUCHOPT asymmetry (2 rejects) ===

# Bare `CREATE ROLE r1` is pinned in auth_basic_extra:
# 93. `CREATE ROLE r2 WITH LOGIN` is pinned in
# auth_basic_extra:98. `CREATE ROLE r REPLICATION` is
# pinned in pg_pubsub_replication_sentinel:240. This
# slice adds the LOGIN PASSWORD composite (parallel to
# auth_basic_extra:98 but with a SECOND attribute
# keyword) and the bare NOLOGIN attribute (covered by
# auth_basic_extra:98 only as `WITH LOGIN`, not the
# negated form). The NOSUCHOPT row pins the parser-
# whitelist reject — DIFFERENT error path from the
# shape (c) above (per Finding 3 in header).

--error 1064
CREATE ROLE r1 LOGIN PASSWORD 'pw';

--error 1064
CREATE ROLE r2 NOLOGIN;

--error 1064
CREATE ROLE r3 NOSUCHOPT;

--echo === PATTERN gamma — ALTER USER / ALTER ROLE shape (c) + NOSUCHOPT asymmetry (6 rejects) ===

# `ALTER USER u IDENTIFIED BY '<pw>'` (MySQL form) is
# pinned in auth_basic_extra:138 with a DIFFERENT
# error message. `ALTER ROLE r WITH PASSWORD '<pw>'`
# is pinned in auth_basic_extra:143. This slice covers
# the OTHER PG ALTER USER / ALTER ROLE variants:
#   - ALTER USER u WITH PASSWORD '<pw>' (PG form,
#     different from the MySQL IDENTIFIED BY form)
#   - ALTER USER u RENAME TO <name>
#   - ALTER USER u SET <param> = <value>
#   - ALTER ROLE r WITH NOLOGIN (different attribute
#     from the PASSWORD form in auth_basic_extra)
#   - ALTER ROLE ALL SET <param> = <value> (PG14+
#     cluster-wide form)
# All hit shape (c). The NOSUCHOPT row pins the SAME
# parser-whitelist asymmetry as PATTERN beta — DIFFERENT
# error path from shape (c).

--error 1064
ALTER USER u1 WITH PASSWORD 'newpw';

--error 1064
ALTER USER u1 RENAME TO u1x;

--error 1064
ALTER USER u1 SET search_path = pg_catalog;

--error 1064
ALTER ROLE r1 WITH NOLOGIN;

--error 1064
ALTER ROLE ALL SET search_path = pg_catalog;

--error 1064
ALTER USER u1 NOSUCHOPT 'x';

--echo === PATTERN delta — DROP ROLE / DROP USER hand-rolled formatters (2 rejects) ===

# Bare `DROP ROLE r1` is pinned in auth_basic_extra:
# 103 and `DROP USER u1` in auth_basic_extra:128 with
# errno 1064 only. THIS slice re-pins both with
# DIFFERENT identifiers AND pins the message-shape
# fingerprint (no `at Line: N, Column: M` suffix) as
# the 4th and 5th witnesses of the
# [[project_hand_rolled_error_formatters]] cluster.
# Both bypass the standard sqlparser error formatter
# and emit bespoke strings. Per the PR #455
# precedent, these are companion bespoke formatters
# to `unsupported DROP STAGE statement` — three of
# the five hand-rolled witnesses are in the DROP
# family.

--error 1064
DROP ROLE r_drop_witness;

--error 1064
DROP USER u_drop_witness;
